  "reason": "The records collectively make the result plausibly reconstructible. R1 and R3 directly establish SPS near 1000–1050 °C as an effective route to dense, hard bulk material in closely related Co–Fe–Ti–Al-containing HEAs, while R5–R7 and R9 show that mechanically alloyed BCC HEA powders consolidate by SPS and commonly develop oxide or other secondary-phase precipitates. R11–R13 supply the key oxidation concepts: diffusion-dependent oxide growth, parabolic or multistage kinetics, and dense protective oxide layers that impede further transport. The corrosion-focused records further connect phase distribution and chloride exposure with localized attack. Although none predicts the exact MgAlTiVFeCo composition, named spinels, or numerical optimum, an expert could reasonably combine these teachings to test SPS around 1000 °C and expect densification plus uniformly distributed oxide barriers to improve hardness, oxidation resistance, and passivation."
